 "finding": "For MoE (O005, O006): at q2 (aggressive uniform quant, otherwise catastrophic -- 0-2/12 battery), targeting ONLY the expert organ at that bit level while protecting attn/router/embed/lm_head at 4-bit rescues capability dramatically (O006: -12 -> -2 delta_hits for +0.07 bpw; O005: -10 -> +1 for +0.08 bpw). By q3+, uniform quantization is ALREADY survivable on its own, and targeted allocation's advantage shrinks to 0-2 hits or vanishes entirely -- it is a rescue mechanism for aggressive compression, not a free lunch at every bit level. mixed-q2q3/q2q4 schemes stay catastrophic on BOTH sides regardless of targeting (q2 present ANYWHERE in a mix still wrecks the model) -- the rule does NOT claim protection from q2 in a mixed scheme, only from PURE uniform single-level q2.\nFor the hybrid negative control (O001): the SAME strategic move (target attn+mlp aggressively, protect the rest) shows NO benefit at any single bit level (every non-mixed pair is EXACTLY tied) and is ACTIVELY WORSE in 2 of 3 mixed configs (mixed-q2q4: uniform 10/12 vs targeted 0/12; mixed-q3q4: uniform 9/12 vs targeted 1/12). The strategy that rescues MoE experts does not transfer to a hybrid architecture's attn/mlp split -- plausibly because MoE experts are individually low-marginal-value and mutually redundant (many near-interchangeable units), while attn+mlp in a hybrid model is a much smaller, more functionally load-bearing set with no equivalent redundancy to exploit.",
